    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's important to avoid using hydrogen peroxide or superglue on open wounds, as both can cause irritation or an allergic reaction and may impede proper healing. Hydrogen peroxide can damage tissue and slow down the healing process, while superglue is not intended for use on living tissue, can harm skin, and should never be applied to a wound. The best approach remains washing the wound with antiseptic soap, applying antibiotic ointment, and covering it with a sterile dressing. If you have concerns about the wound being exposed to dirt while working, consider taking a short break to dress your foot again before resuming work. Additionally, you can wrap the area with plastic wrap to keep out debris, but ensure there's a small part of the injury exposed to the air for proper healing processes. If you notice signs of infection, seek medical advice. Prioritizing health over haste in the long run leads to fewer complications and discomfort.",
